UID:4b75710f6119c9c4@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261118
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor obtains court order re
 quiring NJ-based  construction contractor to pay $2.2M in back wages\, dam
 ages\, penalties
DESCRIPTION:ENGLEWOOD CLIFFS\, NJ – The U.S. Department of Labor has obt
 ained a consent judgment requiring a construction contractor to pay more t
 han $2 million in back wages and damages to 323 construction workers.  
    Entered Nov. 16\, 2026\, in the U.S. District Court for the District
  of New Jersey\, the consent judgment resolves the department’s litigati
 on alleging Premier Concrete Systems LLC and its owners\, Peter Tiflinsky 
 and Antonio Escada\, violated overtime and recordkeeping provisions of the
  Fair Labor Standards Act.The litigation was a result of an investigation 
 by the department’s Wage and Hour Division that found the company and it
 s owners paid employees cash at straight-time rates for all hours worked a
 nd failed to pay them time and one-half their regular rate of pay for hour
 s over 40 in a workweek as required by the FLSA. “Construction workers 
 play a critical role across the country in ensuring safe and reliable stru
 ctures. The Wage and Hour Division is committed to ensuring that construct
 ion workers are paid in compliance with federal labor laws\,” said Wage 
 and Hour Division Regional Administrator James Cain in Philadelphia.The ju
 dgment requires Premier Concrete Systems to pay 323 employees back wages t
 otaling $1\,040\,648 and an equal amount in damages. The judgment also req
 uires the defendants to pay a civil penalty of $118\,704 and prohibits def
 endants from violating the FLSA in the future.“The resolution of this ca
 se is a clear reminder for employers in the construction industry to remem
 ber their responsibilities under the law\,” said Regional Solicitor of L
 abor Jeffrey S. Rogoff in New York City.Employees and employers can contac
 t the division with questions and requests for compliance assistance at it
 s toll-free helpline\, 866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged t
 o use the agency’s industry-specific compliance assistance toolkits to l
 earn about their responsibilities under the laws enforced by the division.
 The agency’s PAID program offers employers an opp\nhttp://www.dol.gov/ne
 wsroom/releases/whd/whd20261118
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261118
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a8cf8239219c196a@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261117
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor issues opinion letters
  addressing Fair Labor Standards Act exemption\, Family and Medical Leave 
 Act hours of service
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour 
 Division today issued two opinion letters that address real-world question
 s related to the Fair Labor Standards Act and the Family and Medical Leave
  Act.The two opinion letters address the applicability of the exemption fo
 r amusement and recreational establishments under the Fair Labor Standard
 s Act and the hours-of-service requirement under the Family and Medical L
 eave Act. Opinion letters provide official written interpretations from t
 he department’s Wage and Hour Division that explain how the laws that t
 he department enforces apply to specific factual circumstances and may hel
 p the public understand their rights and responsibilities. The division is
  committed to providing opinion letters that help American workers and bus
 inesses understand how the FLSA and FMLA apply to their situations.   Th
 e two opinion letters issued today are:FLSA2026-14: Whether the exemption
  for seasonal amusement and recreational establishments in section 13(a)(3
 ) of the FLSA applies to a concessionaire that provides food and drinks at
  events such as a state fair and concerts.FMLA2026-3: Whether time spent w
 riting mandatory reports outside of scheduled work hours and traveling bet
 ween worksites counts as hours of service under the FMLA. In 2025\, the d
 epartment announced the relaunch of the opinion letter program\, which ex
 pands its longstanding commitment to providing meaningful compliance assis
 tance that helps workers\, employers\, and other stakeholders understand h
 ow federal labor laws apply in specific workplace circumstances.Visit the 
 Wage and Hour Division’s opinion letters page to explore past guidance
  and find information on how to submit a request for an opinion letter.Emp
 loyees and employers can contact the Wage and Hour Division with questions
  and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-free helpline\, 866-4
 US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged to use the agency’s industr
 y-specific compliance assistance toolkits\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/re
 leases/whd/whd20261117
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261117
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bee3100f3d7fc8de@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261117
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor proposes new class exe
 mption for qualified professional asset managers to protect retirement pla
 ns from disruptions
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Employee Benef
 its Security Administration today proposed a class exemption that would st
 reamline the process for qualified professional asset managers to continue
  engaging in transactions permitted under Prohibited Transaction Class Exe
 mption 84-14 – also known as the QPAM exemption – following an ineligi
 bility event. The proposed QPAM Re-eligibility Options After Disqualifica
 tion Class Exemption\, known as QROAD\, contains safeguards to protect pla
 ns and participants through clear\, well-established conditions\, includin
 g the requirement that asset managers seeking relief under the proposed ex
 emption have an independent fiduciary certify their eligibility and compli
 ance.“For years\, QPAMs seeking to rely on the beneficial QPAM class exe
 mption following an ineligibility event have had to go through an individu
 al exemption process that can be costly\, burdensome\, and time-consuming 
 for everyone involved\,” said U.S. Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling. 
 “The QROAD class exemption addresses that problem by creating an alterna
 tive path to relief while maintaining the high standards of integrity need
 ed to protect affected plans and participants.”Since 1984\, the QPAM exe
 mption has provided broad relief for employee benefit plan and IRA transac
 tions that otherwise would be prohibited under the Employee Retirement Inc
 ome Security Act if the transactions involved a qualified professional ass
 et manager. QPAMs are expected to maintain a high standard of integrity. 
 If the QPAM or certain of its related parties engage in ineligibility even
 ts\, such as a criminal conviction or prohibited misconduct\, it cannot re
 ly on the QPAM exemption for 10 years. The department has granted several
  individual exemptions over the years that allow QPAMs to continue to rely
  on the QPAM exemption notwithstanding such disqualification. For such exe
 mptions\, QPAMs must demonstrate that their continued reliance on the QPAM
  exemption would protect and benefit plans an\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom
 /releases/ebsa/ebsa20261117
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261117
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fab5f5ad43db4a30@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor proposes to modernize 
 permanent labor certification recruitment\, strengthening protections for 
 American workers
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today issued a pro
 posed rule that would enhance American worker protections when employers
  apply for certification to hire foreign workers permanently in the Unite
 d States. The proposed rule modernizes the Permanent Labor Certification
  program by strengthening recruitment standards and recordkeeping requirem
 ents\, eliminating administrative exemptions to the statutory labor market
  test\, and improving safeguards for all American workers\, especially tho
 se affected by layoffs. This proposed update to the PERM program – the 
 first comprehensive set of regulatory reforms to the program in nearly 20 
 years – will ensure that highly qualified American workers receive fair 
 consideration and first access to job opportunities.“American Workers ar
 e the best in the world\, and they deserve the first and best job opportun
 ities. Existing regulations have instead allowed companies to sponsor fore
 ign workers without fully considering American workers first\,” said Sec
 retary of Labor Keith Sonderling. “This proposed rule change will ensure
  American workers are first in line for high-skilled\, high-paying job opp
 ortunities over foreign labor.”The proposed rule would require employers
  to level the playing field when recruiting workers by using the same chan
 nels and methods when advertising jobs. Proposed changes include a require
 ment to replace print-based job advertisements with online recruiting thro
 ugh employer websites\, online job boards\, social media\, job fairs\, and
  campus recruitment. Employers would also be required to advertise positio
 ns for at least 90 calendar days and demonstrate that they made good-faith
  efforts to recruit qualified American workers. Under the proposal\, emplo
 yers would be prohibited from tailoring job opportunities to foreign worke
 rs. The department also proposed to eliminate the administrative exemptio
 n employers used to bypass the statutory labor market test for certain occ
 upations. Under the proposal\, employers recrui\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsro
 om/releases/eta/eta20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:43035fbd6a725408@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor cites powder coating c
 ompany for willful toxic substance exposures\, repeat violations\, propose
 s more than $1.6M in penalties
DESCRIPTION:HOUSTON – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a powder 
 coating and furniture restoration company for willfully and repeatedly ex
 posing workers to toxic substances and other safety and health hazards.The
  department’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration conducted th
 ree inspections of Outdoor Furniture Refinishing Inc. – doing business a
 s Allied Powder Coating – on April 10\, 2026. One inspection addressed c
 omplaint items after the employer provided an inadequate response to an in
 quiry\, and two follow-up inspections were initiated in relation to safety
  and health violations found during a December 2023 inspection. The employ
 er’s failure to submit abatement documentation prompted a comprehensive 
 review of ongoing hazards.OSHA cited the company for three willful violati
 ons for exposing workers to hazardous levels of 1\,3\,5-triglycidyl isocya
 nurate and total dust during powder coating operations. The agency also is
 sued 18 serious violations for failing to provide training on excessive no
 ise exposure and for deficiencies involving respiratory protection\, hazar
 d communication\, personal protective equipment\, forklift operations\, el
 ectrical safety\, and portable ladders.In addition\, OSHA cited the compan
 y for 18 repeat violations related to respiratory protection\, noise overe
 xposure\, spray booths\, hazard communication\, compressed air\, and elect
 rical hazards. The agency also issued one other-than-serious violation for
  unsanitary working conditions.OSHA has proposed $1\,640\,888 in penalties
 .The agency found that\, despite multiple inspections since 2013 and a for
 mal settlement agreement finalized in December 2024\, the company has fail
 ed to take meaningful action to correct recurring violations.The employer 
 has 15 business days from receipt of its citations and penalties to comply
 \, request an informal conference with OSHA’s area director\, or contest
  the findings before the independent Occupational Safety and Health Review
  Commission. Check the OSHA establishment search p\nhttp://www.dol.gov/n
 ewsroom/releases/osha/osha20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a4edf065f9b1c458@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor applauds federal appea
 ls court decision on administrative exhaustion requirement under federal b
 enefits law
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor applauded a federa
 l appeals court decision allowing employees to bring legal claims directly
  to federal court if they reasonably suspect fiduciary violations in their
  employee benefit plans covered under the Employee Retirement Income Secur
 ity Act.Issued Oct. 13 by the en banc 11th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals\,
  the unanimous decision in Bolton v. Inland Fresh Seafood overturns preced
 ent that required employees to exhaust all administrative remedies under t
 heir ERISA-covered employee benefit plans before they could go to federal 
 court. “This decision vindicates the department’s reinvigorated amicu
 s program and its focus on statutory text\,” said Solicitor of Labor Jon
 athan Berry. “ERISA neither explicitly nor implicitly requires hard-work
 ing Americans to go anywhere but federal court to vindicate their statutor
 y rights under it. By overruling its precedent that erroneously held other
 wise\, the 11th Circuit joins all but one of its sister circuits and reinf
 orces ERISA’s uniform statutory framework.”The court held that the exh
 austion requirement was not based on ERISA’s text\, saying it was an “
 atextual product of judicial creation based solely on policy reasons.” T
 he court’s opinion also quoted the department’s oral argument\, saying
  “plaintiffs or employers should not be able to contract away or force t
 heir employees to contract away their rights under ERISA.” “The decis
 ion by the 11th Circuit rightly rejects a judicially invented exhaustion 
 requirement that has no basis in ERISA’s text or Congressional intent\,
 ” said Assistant Secretary of Labor for the Employee Benefits Security A
 dministration Daniel Aronowitz. “By aligning with the law’s clear text
  and Congress's intent to provide participants and beneficiaries direct ac
 cess to federal court\, the court’s decision makes certain that ERISA’
 s protections are applied as written in the statute itself.”Employers an
 d workers can contact EBSA at askebsa.dol.gov or call 866-444-3272 to\nht
 tp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e443dc9296501983@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Labor Department cites New Jersey pastry m
 aker\, staffing agency for endangering workers after 2 injuries in 3 month
 s\; proposes $351K in fines
DESCRIPTION:PEDRICKTOWN\, NJ – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a 
 pastry manufacturer and a staffing agency for endangering employees by exp
 osing them to hazardous energy and amputation hazards.Investigators with t
 he Occupational Safety and Health Administration responded to an April 28 
 incident at Banneton Bakery LLC and found that an employee provided to the
  bakery by Idalia’s Staffing Agency was cleaning a moving conveyor belt 
 when their arm got stuck\, requiring medical treatment.During the investig
 ation into the April 2026 incident\, OSHA learned of a similar event that 
 occurred on Jan. 16\, 2026\, in which an employee was cleaning the same co
 nveyor belt and suffered a broken arm because the machinery was not locked
  out during the cleaning.The agency cited Banneton for two willful violati
 ons for failing to develop and use lockout/tagout procedures and failing t
 o provide adequate training to ensure employees understood the purpose and
  function of energy control programs. The bakery also received citations f
 or three serious violations for neglecting to ensure side rails of a ladde
 r extended at least 42 inches above the top access level\, not protecting 
 workers from falling through a ladderway floor hole\, and not using group 
 lockout devices properly.In addition\, OSHA cited Idalia’s Staffing Agen
 cy with a serious violation for not ensuring temporary employees received 
 training on using lockout/tagout procedures.The agency proposed $340\,479 
 in penalties for Banneton Bakery and $10\,641 in penalties for Idalia’s 
 Staffing Agency.The employers have 15 business days from receipt of the ci
 tations and penalties to comply\, request an informal conference with OSHA
 ’s area director\, or contest the findings before the independent Occupa
 tional Safety and Health Review Commission. Please check the OSHA establis
 hment search page periodically for any changes in the inspection or penalt
 y status.Visit OSHA’s website to learn about controlling hazardous energ
 y. Employers can also contact the agen\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releas
 es/osha/osha20261116-0
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261116-0
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cf1c4faaa1602e93@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172608Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261109
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Calendar Year 2027 Home Health Prospective Pa
 yment System (HH PPS) Rate Update\; Requirements for the HH Quality Report
 ing Program and the Expanded HH Value-Based Purchasing Model\; Medicare Pr
 ovider Enrollment\, Durable Medical Equipment (DME)\, and DME\, Prosthetic
 s\, Orthotics\, and Supplies (DMEPOS) Policies
DESCRIPTION:This final rule sets forth routine updates to the Medicare hom
 e health payment rates in accordance with existing statutory and regulator
 y requirements. In addition\, this final rule discusses the behavior adjus
 tments and finalizes a temporary behavior adjustment. It also finalizes th
 e recalibrated case-mix weights and updated functional impairment levels\;
  comorbidity subgroups\; and low-utilization payment adjustment (LUPA) thr
 esholds for CY 2027. Additionally\, this final rule discusses and responds
  to comments on the provision of home health palliative care services and 
 includes a summary of comments received on the request for information (RF
 I) on a home health specific wage index included in the proposed rule. Thi
 s rule also finalizes changes to the Home Health Quality Reporting Program
  (HH QRP) and summarizes potential initiatives to improve alignment betwee
 n the HH QRP and expanded Home Health Value- Based Purchasing (HHVBP) Mode
 l. Lastly\, the rule clarifies the application of the DMEPOS face-to- face
  encounter requirements for the replacement of DMEPOS items\; makes change
 s to the provider and supplier enrollment requirements\; makes changes to 
 implement a DME benefit expansion for infusion pumps and drugs\; and discu
 sses collection of information requirement changes regarding the DMEPOS Co
 mpetitive Bidding Program (CBP) country of origin.\nhttps://www.federalreg
 ister.gov/documents/2026/11/09/2026-22750/calendar-year-2027-home-health-p
 rospective-payment-system-hh-pps-rate-update-requirements-for-the-hh
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/11/09/2026-22750/calend
 ar-year-2027-home-health-prospective-payment-system-hh-pps-rate-update-req
 uirements-for-the-hh
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5f7121b1ab084310@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172608Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Prohibiting Illegal Discrimination in Registe
 red Apprenticeship Programs
DESCRIPTION:The Department of Labor (DOL or the Department) is issuing thi
 s final rule to remove the onerous and legally questionable affirmative ac
 tion requirements that were previously imposed by the Department on Regist
 ered Apprenticeship program sponsors. This final rule also streamlines and
  simplifies nondiscrimination obligations for all program sponsors\, while
  clarifying that sponsors are accountable for compliance with Federal and 
 State nondiscrimination protections. It also makes conforming\, technical 
 changes to the Department's regulation that addresses Labor Standards for 
 the Registration of Apprenticeship Programs.\nhttps://www.federalregister.
 gov/documents/2026/11/06/2026-22680/prohibiting-illegal-discrimination-in-
 registered-apprenticeship-programs
